Chapter: IdmKeyTests

  cites Kernel chapter IdentityManager

 First runtime coverage for the identity path.

 codex/os/kernel/IdentityManager.codex is cited by exactly one chapter
 (apps/works/FirstBoot) and no battery compiled either, so it sat broken,
 then merely compiling, and nothing ever executed a line of it. This is
 the chapter behind CurrentPlan gap 2.

 The ceremony as a whole is not deterministic -- idm-collect-keyboard-entropy
 mixes get-ticks into the seed -- but its cryptography is: every function
 below is a pure function of its arguments, and the entropy only decides
 what arguments they get. So the crypto is testable today, and it is the
 half that was wrong.

 idm-derive-key must return 32 BYTES. That is the whole of the first
 assertion and it is not a detail: this derivation was written out at all
 four sites that need it and ran hkdf's result through
 idm-hash-words-to-bytes at every one. hkdf returns bytes -- hkdf-expand
 converts each hmac block and takes `length` of them, and
 apps/hkdf-vector-test pins that against RFC 5869 -- so expanding again
 turned each byte b into [0,0,0,b]: a 128-element key, 97 zeros, of which
 AES-256 takes the first 32. Eight bytes of real entropy in a 256-bit key.

 Nothing could see it, and this is why the test asserts a LENGTH and a
 zero-count rather than an encrypt/decrypt round trip. The same wrong key
 was used to encrypt at first boot and to decrypt at unlock, so the
 ceremony round-tripped perfectly: the passphrase worked, the key loaded,
 and the only symptom was a number nobody read. A round trip agrees with
 itself. Ask a question it cannot answer by agreeing.

Section: Definitions

  count-zeros : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-zeros (xs) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else if list-at xs i == 0 then count-zeros xs (i + 1) len (acc + 1)
    else count-zeros xs (i + 1) len acc

 A 32-byte key, not 128. The buggy form answered 128.

  test-derive-length : Integer
  test-derive-length =
    if list-length (idm-derive-key [1, 2, 3] [4, 5, 6]) == 32 then 1 else 0

 And it must look like a key rather than like a byte smeared over four
 slots. The buggy 128-element form was 97 zeros; a real derivation over
 these fixed inputs has few. Fixed inputs, so this is exact, not
 statistical.

  test-derive-not-smeared : Integer
  test-derive-not-smeared =
    let k = idm-derive-key [1, 2, 3] [4, 5, 6]
    in if count-zeros k 0 (list-length k) 0 <= 4 then 1 else 0

 Changing the salt must change the key. A derivation that ignored an
 argument would pass both tests above.

  test-salt-matters : Integer
  test-salt-matters =
    if idm-bytes-eq (idm-derive-key [1, 2, 3] [4, 5, 6]) (idm-derive-key [9, 9, 9] [4, 5, 6]) then 0 else 1

  test-passphrase-matters : Integer
  test-passphrase-matters =
    if idm-bytes-eq (idm-derive-key [1, 2, 3] [4, 5, 6]) (idm-derive-key [1, 2, 3] [7, 7, 7]) then 0 else 1

 idm-generate-keypair is a pure function of its seed: it takes 32 bytes
 and asks Ed25519 for the public key. Same seed, same keypair -- and a
 different seed had better not give the same one.

  test-keypair-deterministic : Integer
  test-keypair-deterministic =
    let a = idm-generate-keypair (idm-hash-words-to-bytes (sha256 [1, 2, 3]))
    in let b = idm-generate-keypair (idm-hash-words-to-bytes (sha256 [1, 2, 3]))
    in if idm-bytes-eq (a.ikp-public) (b.ikp-public) then 1 else 0

  test-keypair-seed-matters : Integer
  test-keypair-seed-matters =
    let a = idm-generate-keypair (idm-hash-words-to-bytes (sha256 [1, 2, 3]))
    in let b = idm-generate-keypair (idm-hash-words-to-bytes (sha256 [4, 5, 6]))
    in if idm-bytes-eq (a.ikp-public) (b.ikp-public) then 0 else 1

 An Ed25519 public key is 32 bytes, and the private half is the seed.

  test-keypair-shape : Integer
  test-keypair-shape =
    let k = idm-generate-keypair (idm-hash-words-to-bytes (sha256 [1, 2, 3]))
    in if list-length (k.ikp-public) == 32 & list-length (k.ikp-private) == 32 then 1 else 0

 idm-take-bytes pads a short source with zeros rather than truncating to
 what it has -- the property first-boot relies on when the hash is
 shorter than the key.

  test-take-pads : Integer
  test-take-pads =
    let t = idm-take-bytes [7, 7] 4
    in if list-length t == 4 & list-at t 2 == 0 & list-at t 3 == 0 then 1 else 0

 idm-bytes-eq is the passphrase comparison. It must not call unequal
 lengths equal, and it must not call a prefix a match.

  test-bytes-eq : Integer
  test-bytes-eq =
    if idm-bytes-eq [1, 2, 3] [1, 2, 3] & (idm-bytes-eq [1, 2, 3] [1, 2] == False) & (idm-bytes-eq [1, 2, 3] [1, 2, 4] == False) then 1 else 0

 idm-zero-list wipes the plaintext buffers on every path out of unlock.
 It must actually zero them.

  test-zero-list : Integer
  test-zero-list =
    let xs = [1, 2, 3, 4]
    in let z = idm-zero-list xs 0
    in if count-zeros xs 0 (list-length xs) 0 == 4 then 1 else 0

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (integer-to-text (test-derive-length + test-derive-not-smeared + test-salt-matters + test-passphrase-matters + test-keypair-deterministic + test-keypair-seed-matters + test-keypair-shape + test-take-pads + test-bytes-eq + test-zero-list))
  end
